{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Constant-Acceleration Equations.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Constant-Acceleration Equations.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"constant acceleration equations constant acceleration equations","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Constant-Acceleration Equations.md","fileName":"Constant-Acceleration Equations.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Constant-Acceleration Equations","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-17 00:04\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Kinematics and Newtonian Motion]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Kinematics and Newtonian Motion","foundationsofphysics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Constant-Acceleration Equations.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Constant-Acceleration Equations.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"constant acceleration equations constant acceleration equations","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Constant-Acceleration Equations.md","fileName":"Constant-Acceleration Equations.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Constant-Acceleration Equations","heading":"Constant-Acceleration Equations","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"For one-dimensional motion with constant acceleration, the variables displacement $s$, initial velocity $u$, final velocity $v$, acceleration $a$, and time $t$ satisfy equations including $v=u+at$, $s=ut+\\tfrac12at^2$, and $v^2=u^2+2as$. Each equation omits one variable, so the known and required quantities determine which form is useful.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Kinematics and Newtonian Motion","foundationsofphysics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Constant-Acceleration Equations.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Constant-Acceleration Equations.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sour